@meeting talk.vance
// SW2, 0.92.6 item 13: Day 74, Thursday 19:00-19:45, a chief's talk in the auditorium seats (the writer's pick: the
// Executive Officer). Commander Teo Vance: "Why every rule is there". Nothing about his messages home or the poems.
title: Why every rule is there
from: Commander Teo Vance
gist: Commander Vance's talk on the regulations: who they protect, and how he learnt them.
== lines
vance: Good evening. Three parts, then questions. Please keep questions until the end.   [nod]
vance: Part one. The Survey Service regulations run to eleven hundred pages. I've read them twice.
vance: Part two. Nearly all of them say one thing: bring everyone home as they left.
vance: No running in the corridors, for example. It isn't about running. It's about corners.
vance: Part three. Every rule is there because someone, once, found out the hard way.
vance: As a cadet, I was usually that someone. That was a joke. Moving on.   [laugh]
vance: Learn the rules, and you'll know which ones let you help. That's the trick. Questions.
? choice
* Which rule did you break first?   rel:vance+2 flavour:cheer mem:talk.vance.asked
  vance: Curfew. Then the one on climbing. Then the one about the galley. Noted, and moving on.   [laugh]
* Which rule lets you help people?   rel:vance+2 flavour:practical mem:talk.vance.asked
  vance: The duty officer may excuse anyone for a call home. I use that one often. Gladly.   [nod]
* (Just listen)
  vance: No questions. Efficient. Thank you.
vance: That concludes the talk. Carry on.
